A name that no tailnet DNS route covers, and whose tailnet queries all fail,
counts as a failed lookup. Upstream then asks the host's resolver, which sends
the name to the device's network and lets that network's DNS answer with any
address, including a reachable tailnet address.

In net/dns/resolver/forwarder.go, getDialerType wraps the selected dialer with
a per-address check. Normalize IP literals and dial them with NetstackDialTCP
when UseNetstackForIP recognizes the route; retain the original dialer for all
other destinations. Android otherwise uses SystemDial for TCP DNS and DoH
unless control enables user-dial-routes, even though UDP DNS already uses
netstack for tailnet resolvers. Calling NetstackDialTCP directly also prevents
a route disappearing between the check and dial from causing device-network
fallback. See https://github.com/tailscale/tailscale/pull/15259.

The same file imports net/tlsdial and sets the DoH TLS configuration's RootCAs
to tlsdial.SystemRoots. This path bypasses tlsdial.Config, so it also needs the
Android certificate pool to avoid filesystem root discovery.

